Renewable energy technologies are of great importance in addressing the energy crisis and global environmental change.[1–4] Among various electrochemical energy storage and conversion systems, hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs) have been widely investigated in recent years because of multiple advantages of high power density, fast charge-discharge rate and excellent cycling stability.[5–7] The battery-type electrode in the HSC is the key component to build an efficient device.[8] As a result, continuous efforts have been dedicated to developing high performance battery-type electrode materials for HSCs. Among the potential electrode materials, transition metal sulfides (TMSs) are regarded as promising materials in view of their high capacitance, rich redox sites and improved conductivity compared with their corresponding metal oxides.[9] Superior to monometal sulfides, mixed metal sulfides by introduction of hetero-metal ions offer enhanced charge transfer between different ions and modified electronic structures to lower the kinetic energy barriers of the electrochemical processes.[8,10]
Designing proper structures for the battery-type electrode materials may greatly promote their electrochemical performance.[10,11] Hollow structures with high surface area, rich active sites, shortened distance for charge transfer and reduced aggregation of nanoscale subunits have been widely used in many energy related applications.[12–16] Among various hollow architectures, complex multi-shelled hollow structures have been demonstrated to exhibit better performance than conventional simple hollow structures.[17–19] Specifically, improved energy densities can be obtained from multi-shelled electrode materials as a result of higher content of active species within the hollow particles. Moreover, interconnection of neighboring shells can also enhance the structural robustness for better cycling stability.[20] As a result, fabrication of electrode materials with multi-shelled structures might be one effective way to enhance the electrochemical performance of HSCs.
